  // Recent releases. An AI reads this when a user asks "what's new?"
  "changelog": [
    {"version": "4.21.5", "date": "2026-09-12", "changes": ["Late-arriving history is not tonight's news. A dormant tenant's months-old hot sessions are archived on its first load, and the session_log memory carried the archival time, so the dreamer read April events as last night's (the 2026-09-12 brief opened with 22 April bus messages). An archived session's memory is now stamped with the session's last exchange, and the dreamer's harvest gates on the memory's own timestamp as well as file mtime, skipping and counting anything older than the window by more than an hour of write-latency grace. Missing or unparseable timestamps keep the mtime verdict."]},
    {"version": "4.21.4", "date": "2026-09-12", "changes": ["Dreamer tone. The nightly brief's Lessons-learned item asked for failures, workarounds and doctrines reinforced, which carried the agents' blame narrative into every boot. All three dreamer prompts now ask for one specimen and one rule per line: what exactly happened, then what to do next time. The concrete trap stays because it is what makes the lesson stick; the story of fault goes. Idea credited to Merlin (burn the trauma, keep the lesson); wording is ours. Prompt-only, no schema or grammar change; the stated-line gate is untouched."]},
    {"version": "4.21.3", "date": "2026-09-10", "changes": ["Ranking, two small fixes. (1) A dotted number run in the prompt (4.20.2, v2026.9.7) is searched as an FTS5 phrase in both spellings instead of dissolving into filler tokens, and three-plus parts is identifier-shaped, so a version the prompt names pins its memory under the same 1-25 document cap (two-part numbers like 3.12 stay topics). No index rebuild. (2) Pinned memories order newest first with memory_id as the tie-break, so the same prompt serves the same pin order until the store changes (the access bump no longer flips near-ties). Stated limit: a newer memory that merely mentions the identifier leads an older one that answers it."]},
    {"version": "4.21.2", "date": "2026-09-10", "changes": ["Durability, two boring fixes. (1) atomic_write_text/atomic_write_bytes now fsync the file before os.replace and the directory after, so an acknowledged memory survives a power cut, not only a process crash (the S06 crash-durability finding; the ledger seal already fsynced). (2) The Cortex Stick courier carries fact_history: facts/fact_history.jsonl is the set-union of every host's origin audit rows (save/demote), identified by content hash, encrypted and manifest-covered like facts.jsonl; the courier's own merge rows stay local. The receiving host can now answer what a fact used to be (the S04 offline-carry finding)."]},
    {"version": "4.21.1", "date": "2026-09-10", "changes": ["Exact identifiers first: a memory holding a rare digit-bearing identifier the prompt named (a commit hash, an advisory id, a CVE, a CVE; identifier-shaped, in at most 25 memories) is served first in focus and recent modes, capped at half the window. Found by the first live smoke on the 17k-row cc tenant: the memory holding a commit hash sat too far below the pool top on cosine for the 4.21.0 lexical bonus to lift it."]},
    {"version": "4.21.0", "date": "2026-09-10", "changes": ["The lexical lane: every /context also runs a BM25 keyword search (SQLite FTS5 table `vec_lex` inside the per-agent vec index, kept in step on every write) over the same text the vectors were built from. A memory the kNN missed joins the pool as a `LEX` chunk scored on the same cosine scale; a small `w_lexical` term (0.10) in the composite lets exact identifiers (advisory ids, error strings, hashes, file names, ports) re-order the on-topic band. Measured: identifier world MRR 0.848 -> 1.000, paraphrase harness recall@5 0.971 -> 1.000 at unchanged MRR. `ranking.lexical_enabled: false` = 4.20 recall. New wire value `cache_tier: LEX`; `cache_hits.LEX`."]},
    {"version": "4.20.2", "date": "2026-09-06", "changes": ["`ledger seal --all`: no read timeout on the per-tenant POST (a first-time adopt of 17k records outran the 120 s cap and the CLI died with a traceback while the server finished the job) and a transport error prints red and continues; `ledger verify --all --json` is pure JSON again — the `skipped` notices for archived tenant dirs go to stderr. Both found by the 4.20.1 seal run on the production host."]},
    {"version": "4.20.1", "date": "2026-09-06", "changes": ["`ledger verify|seal --all` skips on-disk tenant directories whose names are not valid agent_ids (archived tenants such as `rocky.archived-20260516`), saying so, instead of aborting the whole run when the server rejects the first one; a non-200 for one tenant during `seal --all` is reported and the run continues, exiting 1 at the end. Found by the 4.20.0 upgrade step on the production host: 26 tenants, run died on tenant 1, nothing sealed."]},
    {"version": "4.20.0", "date": "2026-09-06", "changes": ["Memory Ledger — a tamper-evident chain over what the store was told. Every save (writeback, Analyst/Muse notes, archived-session indexing) seals the memory's TESTIMONY (id, session, agent, summary, key_facts, decisions, projects, timestamp) into an append-only per-tenant `ledger.jsonl`: SHA-256 of the sealed fields, the previous entry's hash, and the entry's own hash. Filing fields (category, tags, superseded_by, reclassification marks) stay mutable by design and do not disturb the seal. New `GET /ledger/verify` walks the chain and checks every sealed record on disk (chain intact/broken/empty; per record sealed/altered/missing/unsealed, plus disputed past a break — the chain is trusted only up to its first break); new `POST /ledger/seal` adopts unsealed records (pre-ledger stores, stick carries), never launders an altered one, and refuses (409) while the chain is broken. Both scopable. CLI `mnemo-cortex ledger verify|seal` (seal probes /health: through the server when it answers, direct to disk when the port refuses, refuses on anything ambiguous). Idea credited to MAYA Memory Lane's sealed blocks; built from zero for Mnemo's per-record store."]},
    {"version": "4.19.0", "date": "2026-09-05", "changes": ["Pocket Mnemo — the phone is the door, the engine stays home. New `POST /chat`: one turn = recall (`/context`, persona-aware) -> reasoning provider -> save (`/writeback`, source=user, tag `pocket`), all inside the caller's tenant; every rule those handlers enforce (scope pin, read-only, capture gate, redaction, dedup hold) applies to the phone, and the save outcome is reported in `save_status`, never dropped silently. `/chat` is scopable, and a scoped token may omit `agent_id` — the pin is the identity, so the phone holds nothing but a token (lost phone = revoke one token). New `/app/`: a phone-sized web app served by the server itself (one origin, no CORS, no new listener, closed asset allowlist, no secrets in the shell) — chat, a 'what I remember' pane showing exactly what the reply was given, a close-the-lid button that forgets the conversation on the phone while the engine keeps it, Neon Arcade skin with the mandatory dimmer. The message and history are redacted before they reach the reasoner; the reply is stored as a labelled key_fact, never as the person's words; pocket recall hides no category, so a turn the classifier files as session_log is still remembered. Tailnet / LAN only by design: nothing new faces the internet."]},
    {"version": "4.18.5", "date": "2026-09-05", "changes": ["CI green again: the Cortex Stick Windows-eject test no longer crashes pytest on Python 3.11. `_eject_stick` built a concrete `Path` before branching on `os.name`; under the cross-host test's fake `os.name = \"nt\"`, 3.11 refuses to instantiate `WindowsPath` off-Windows and pytest then died building its own `Path` while reporting the failure (INTERNALERROR, six red runs since 4.18.0). The Windows branch now uses `PureWindowsPath` only; the test scopes its patch so a future failure reports as a failure. No behaviour change on any real host."]},
    {"version": "4.18.4", "date": "2026-09-05", "changes": ["Vectors are unit-normalised at the index boundary (insert and query), and an index written before this by a non-normalising provider is scaled in place once on open: the L2-to-cosine mapping assumed unit vectors, and fastembed nomic (norm ~20) collapsed every pooled similarity to 1.0 — similarity inert. Revision order: inside the served window a memory forced past the dedup gate (`near_dup_of`) is served before the memory it was held against — the stale truth still serves, after the new one; membership is unchanged. New recall lens `recent` (the boot lens): the pool is the kNN neighbours plus the newest rows, similarity gates the on-topic band, the date orders; shows session_log unless the caller excludes it. Found by the memory proving ground (S02 ranker-current, S03 latest-present)."]},
    {"version": "4.18.3", "date": "2026-09-04", "changes": ["Writeback: the memory id now mixes in the content (was session_id:timestamp alone, so two writes sharing a stamp — every import, stick carry and backfill — silently overwrote each other); future-dated timestamps clamp to now; an unparseable timestamp is flagged `created_at_fallback` on the record and logged; `ingested_at` records arrival and the rulekeeper dedup window reads it, so backdated imports are scanned."]},
    {"version": "4.18.2", "date": "2026-09-04", "changes": ["Writeback: a caller-supplied `timestamp` is the memory's birth — `created_at` in the record and the vec index now derive from it (was always now), so imports, carries and backfills age, rank, stale-warn and dedup by their real age."]},
    {"version": "4.18.1", "date": "2026-09-04", "changes": ["Cortex Stick on Windows: the eject actually fires — the shell offers the verb as `E&ject`, so `InvokeVerb('Eject')` matched nothing while returning 0; now the verbs are enumerated, matched with the accelerator stripped, and invoked with DoIt(); an absent verb is a loud non-zero exit."]},
    {"version": "4.18.0", "date": "2026-09-04", "changes": ["Cortex Stick: `stick watch` ejects the stick after a verified sync (udisksctl / diskutil / Shell.Application Eject), so 'safe to remove' is literal and a FAT volume never leaves with its dirty bit set; success is verified by the volume being gone, an eject failure toasts `EJECT FAILED: unmount by hand`; `--no-eject` keeps the old stay-mounted re-sync, `stick sync --eject` opts a manual sync in."]},
    {"version": "4.17.4", "date": "2026-09-04", "changes": ["Cortex Stick: `stick watch` survives an unexpected sync error (an unreadable host file, a missing codec dependency) — traceback to the log, a `sync FAILED, nothing moved` toast to the human, then it waits for the next plug-in instead of dying. Before, the only signal was the absence of a toast."]},
    {"version": "4.17.3", "date": "2026-09-04", "changes": ["Cortex Stick: the watcher toast works on Windows (WinRT toast, no module install), so both desks of a two-desk setup can be plug-and-walk-away; a host with no brain repo configured now reports `brain: not configured` instead of `skipped`."]},
    {"version": "4.17.2", "date": "2026-09-04", "changes": ["Cortex Stick: stick discovery skips a mount it cannot read instead of crashing the watcher; a root-owned sibling drive (mid-mount, autofs stub) used to kill `stick watch` with PermissionError until systemd restarted it."]},
    {"version": "4.17.1", "date": "2026-09-03", "changes": ["/context refuses an empty or whitespace-only prompt with 422 (min_length + validator on ContextRequest.prompt); it previously returned 200 and, in explore mode, a chunk recalled against nothing."]},
    {"version": "4.17.0", "date": "2026-09-02", "changes": ["Business / artist mode: a /context call that names no mode takes its recall lens from the persona (strict/default -> focus, creative -> explore); new top-level default_persona config key is the server-wide switch, validated at load; /health reports it; every /context response names the lens that served in a new mode field."]},
    {"version": "4.16.0", "date": "2026-09-02", "changes": ["VEC-only recall pool (HOT/L1/L2 retired; L1Cache/L2Index deleted); pool-normalised similarity (Experiment One); explore mode rescaled to span-fraction constants via its own harness (E4); /preflight on VEC; recall harness gates every ranker change (E2)."]},
    {"version": "4.15.0", "date": "2026-08-01", "changes": ["Bounded write-time near-duplicate detection: top-5 same-tenant vector candidates confirmed by measured token overlap; interactive holds with force/supersede resolution, non-interactive loss-proof saves, allowlists, and advisory-only nightly rulekeeper reporting into Dreamer + bus."]},
    {"version": "4.14.0", "date": "2026-07-13", "changes": ["ChatGPT integration — a Custom GPT gets memory you own via two REST Actions (recallMemory/saveMemory) through a hardened gate (integrations/chatgpt/): bearer-authenticated, pinned to a single memory tenant no matter what the caller claims, source=user + chatgpt-gate tag forced on saves, 10/hour rate limit, 8KB body caps, category allowlist, audit log, generic errors. The Mnemo server and its API key never face the internet — publish only the gate (Tailscale Funnel or any HTTPS proxy). Full guide: docs/install-chatgpt.md. OpenAI tier note: Custom GPT Actions need Plus+; full custom MCP save+recall in the main ChatGPT app needs Business/Enterprise (Plus MCP connectors are read-only)."]},
    {"version": "4.13.0", "date": "2026-07-12", "changes": ["Cortex Stick zero-terminal courier: `stick watch --notify` raises a desktop toast on every sync (files/facts/brain counts, 'safe to remove') and — critically — on SYNC REFUSED, so unattended plug-in syncing under a systemd user unit or Task Scheduler never hides a stick that needs a human. notify-send (Linux) / osascript (macOS), silent no-op elsewhere; the console line stays the source of truth."]},
    {"version": "4.12.0", "date": "2026-07-12", "changes": ["Cortex Stick facts channel — the structured Facts store now travels too. The stick carries a canonical JSONL export of facts.sqlite (encrypted like everything else); hosts merge row-wise under ladder-respecting rules: verified survives a newer high_probability contradiction, a newer demotion (confidence='false') propagates while a stale one loses to re-establishment, same-value reasserts merge to max confidence. No deletes exist (demotion IS the tombstone), the merge is commutative and idempotent, and every courier-applied change writes a fact_history audit row. Config: facts: false in {data_dir}/stick.json to opt out."]},
    {"version": "4.11.1", "date": "2026-07-12", "changes": ["Windows fix: the courier's JSONL union-merge crashed on non-ASCII characters ('→') because text writes used the platform default encoding (cp1252). All truth-file text writes now pin UTF-8; regression-tested under a real non-UTF-8 locale subprocess."]},
    {"version": "4.11.0", "date": "2026-07-12", "changes": ["Cortex Stick encryption — a lost stick is no longer a lost memory. AES-256-SIV file crypto in the courier itself (works identically on Windows/Linux/macOS, keeps FAT32/exFAT, no admin rights, no volume drivers). The key derives from a passphrase (scrypt) and is stored per-host, NEVER on the stick; a wrong passphrase fails loud via a passport fingerprint. Deterministic SIV keeps the whole 3-way merge working in ciphertext space — torn-generation detection and `stick repair` still work without the key, but content never decrypts without it (tamper = loud AEAD refusal). The brain travels as an encrypted git bundle instead of a readable bare repo. New CLI: `stick init --encrypt`, `stick unlock` (enroll a host), `stick encrypt` (in-place, resumable migration of a plaintext stick), `stick brain-clone`. Needs the 'cryptography' package: pip install 'mnemo-cortex[stick-crypto]'."]},
    {"version": "4.10.0", "date": "2026-07-12", "changes": ["Cortex Stick — a USB stick as a courier between two full Mnemo installations (home + work). Carries the delta: memory JSONs, trajectory JSONLs, an optional brain git repo, and a free-form project pad. No cloud, no VPN. State-based 3-way merge (no tombstones, no schema changes), plan-pass-before-mutation so a refused sync changes nothing, torn-generation detection with an in-tool `stick repair` recovery, conflict losers always preserved. CLI: mnemo-cortex stick init/sync/status/repair/watch. v1 is plaintext by design."]},
    {"version": "4.5.0", "date": "2026-06-25", "changes": ["Trajectory Learning Phase 1 — two new tools (mnemo_save_trajectory / mnemo_recall_trajectory) let an agent capture the step-by-step recipe of a task that went well and recall the nearest proven recipes before a similar task (ranked by similarity, then self-rating, then recency). Per-agent append-only JSONL + sqlite-vec index; explicit save, no auto-capture, no cross-agent sharing in Phase 1."]},
    {"version": "4.4.1", "date": "2026-06-23", "changes": ["Native Windows support — the server now runs as native Windows Python (no WSL). Passport file locking is cross-platform: POSIX fcntl with an msvcrt fallback. Verified: agentb.server imports and serves recall on Windows."]},
    {"version": "4.1.0", "date": "2026-06-10", "changes": ["Secret redaction on every write path — printed API keys never reach the store.", "Capture pause gate with dead-man auto-resume for sensitive operations.", "Composite recall ranking: similarity + recency + category importance + access frequency.", "The Analyst — distills raw session logs into first-class notes (decisions, incidents, doctrines) automatically.", "Deleted memories no longer resurrect through caches; L2 write path retired; real auth probes for all providers."]},
    {"version": "4.0.0", "date": "2026-06-09", "changes": ["Smart Ingestion — LLM classification at save time, reclassification migration CLI, dreamer reclassify pass. Two-tier recall: smart notes surface by default, raw session logs stay reachable but hidden. (4.0.1–4.0.3: recall tier-filter fixes + real OpenRouter health probe.)"]},
    {"version": "3.3.0", "date": "2026-05-30", "changes": ["Public Facts seeder — seed the Facts table from a YAML of canonical truths so exact lookups don't lose to stale recall. Opt-in install + git hook + nightly cron."]},
    {"version": "3.2.0", "date": "2026-05-29", "changes": ["Dreamer git-sync check — the nightly run flags when a watched repo has drifted (dirty, unpushed, or behind), so silent staleness gets caught."]},
